.TH KILL 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
kill \- send a signal to a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kill
.RB [ \-s
.IR signal " | "
.BI \- signal ]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.IR pid ...
.br
.B kill \-l
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kill
sends a signal to each
.IR pid ,
.B SIGTERM
unless another is given.
A negative
.I pid
is a process group: every process in it gets the signal.
Give it after
.B \-\-
so it isn't read as a signal.
.PP
A signal is a name, with or without its
.B SIG
prefix, in any case (\fB\-KILL\fR, \fB\-sigint\fR), or its number
(\fB\-9\fR).
The signals mcos knows are
.B SIGHUP
(1),
.B SIGINT
(2),
.B SIGKILL
(9),
.B SIGPIPE
(13),
.B SIGTERM
(15) and
.B SIGCHLD
(17).
.B SIGKILL
cannot be caught or ignored: the process ends at once and runs none of
its code.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR \fIsignal\fR, \fB\-\fIsignal\fR
The signal to send.
.TP
.B \-l
Lists the signals, by number.
.TP
.B \-\-
Ends the options: what follows is pids.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if every signal was sent; 1 if any failed (no such process, a pid
that isn't a number, an unknown signal); 2 with no pid.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
kill 12
kill \-9 12 13
kill \-s INT 7
kill \-TERM \-\- \-5
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR sh (1)
